{ id:'enthd-nose-316', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-epistaxis',
  stem:'Which of the following is a factor that **contributes to epistaxis**?',
  options:['Thrombocytopenia','Nasal polyps','Septal spur','All of the above','None of the above'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**All of the above — the lecture’s own aetiological grid has a slot for each of the three, and the question is really asking whether you have read the whole grid rather than only the headline.**\n\n`L25.1) epistaxis.pdf` sorts the causes into four families: *"I : Idiopathic. II : Local factors. III : Systemic factors. IV : Environmental."* The three options are drawn one from each of the two families that matter here.\n\n| Option | Where the deck files it | Mechanism |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Thrombocytopenia** | **systemic — bleeding disorders**, printed by name: *"Thrombocytopenia (hypersplenism, DIC)"*, alongside *"Coagulopathies (hemophilia, anticoagulants)"*, *"Platelet dysfunction (leukemia, aspirin, Von Willebrand’s)"* and *"Vasculitis (Osler-Weber)"* | too few platelets, so a trivial mucosal breach does not seal |\n| **Nasal polyps** | **local**, under *"Neoplasm"* and the masses list of `L26) nasal masses.pdf`, which prints ***"Bleeding polyp"*** as a named entity in the unilateral endonasal/septal differential | a vascular polypoid mass ulcerates and bleeds on contact |\n| **Septal spur** | **local — "Structural"**, one of the nine local headings the deck lists | Grade Gain gives the exact mechanism: *"stretching of the mucosa over the septal spur"* (`entqb-nose2-82`) — the mucosa over a sharp spur is thinned, dried by the airstream deflected across it, and crusts and cracks |\n\n**Why the two totalising options are not interchangeable.** *None of the above* is refuted by any single row of that table, so the only live choice is between one named factor and *all of the above*; when three plausible items all appear in the same printed aetiology, the inclusive option is the one the examiner wants.\n\n**⚠️ One caution that is worth carrying past this question, and it is not the bank’s point:** a polyp that **bleeds** is not an ordinary inflammatory polyp. `L26)` puts *"Bleeding polyp"* in the **unilateral** column, and its own alarm list reads *"Persistent unilateral progressive nasal obstruction · Persistent unilateral watery rhinorrhea · **Persistent unilateral epistaxis** · Persistent unilateral sinus pain · Extranasal manifestations"*. Ordinary bilateral nasal polyposis is pale, insensitive and avascular and does **not** bleed; a bleeding unilateral mass means angiofibroma, inverted papilloma or malignancy until proved otherwise.\n\n**Bottom line: epistaxis is a symptom with a four-family aetiology, and local structural, local neoplastic and systemic haematological causes all sit inside it.** Cross-references: `entqb-nose2-56` (idiopathic is the single commonest cause overall), `entep-nose-61` (idiopathic in children too), `entqb-nose2-82`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Epistaxis has four aetiological families — idiopathic, local, systemic and environmental — so a septal spur (local structural), a bleeding polyp (local neoplastic) and thrombocytopenia (systemic) all contribute.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.92. **Printed inside the bank’s rhinosinusitis run but an epistaxis question by content**, so filed to `ent-epistaxis`. The four-family aetiology, the nine local headings and the bleeding-disorder list are transcribed from `L25.1) epistaxis.pdf`; *"Bleeding polyp"* and the unilateral alarm list from `L26) nasal masses.pdf`. The spur mechanism is Grade Gain’s `entqb-nose2-82` rather than outside knowledge.' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-317', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-sinusitis',
  stem:'A **4 year old boy** has **bilateral nasal obstruction, mucoid nasal discharge and fever (38.3C) for the past 3 days**. His mother has tried some OTC medications, but she reports minimal improvement and has recently noted that **the discharge has become discolored**. Examination revealed **marked nasal congestion and pharyngeal hyperemia**. What is the **best next step**?',
  options:['Assurance and symptomatic treatment','Culture of nasal discharge','X-ray nose and PNS','CT nose and PNS','Empirical antibiotic therapy against common pathogens'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**Assurance and symptomatic treatment — this is day 3 of an ordinary viral rhinosinusitis, and not one of the four features that mark bacterial disease is present.**\n\n`L27.1) ACUTE rhinosinusitis.pdf` prints the discriminator as a list, and it is the whole question:\n\n> *"Factors suggesting acute bacterial sinusitis are: • **Worsening of the symptoms after five days.** • **Persistence of symptoms for more than 10 days.** • **Presence of symptoms out of proportion** to those typically associated with viral URTI. • **Worsening of symptoms after initial improvement (“double-sickening”)**."*\n\nThree days, no plateau-then-relapse, no disproportion. The same lecture supplies the base rates that make the odds overwhelming: *"Approximately 1 billion cases of viral rhinosinusitis occur each year in the United States; of these, only **0.5% to 2.0%** are complicated by bacterial infection of the sinuses."*\n\n**⚠️ THE DISCOLOURED DISCHARGE IS THE TRAP, AND IT IS THE SINGLE COMMONEST REASON A CHILD IS GIVEN AN ANTIBIOTIC HE DOES NOT NEED.** Colour is **not** on the deck’s list. `L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf` prints the natural history of the common cold as *"Watery rhinorrhea"* followed by *"**Mucopurulent Rhinorrhea**"* and then *"Resolution within 5-10 days"* — the change of colour is a **stage of the normal illness**, not evidence of bacteria (`entqb-nose4-167`).\n\n| Option | Why it is wrong here |\n|---|---|\n| **Culture of nasal discharge** | `L27.1` restricts culture to four settings — *"Recurrent acute rhinosinusitis, acute exacerbation of chronic rhinosinusitis, immunosuppression, failure to respond to appropriate initial antibiotic therapy"* — and none applies. A swab of the nasal cavity samples commensal flora anyway; the meaningful specimen is endoscopic, from the middle meatus |\n| **X-ray nose and PNS** | plain films cannot separate viral from bacterial mucosal thickening and are not part of the deck’s diagnostic triad of *"History · Clinical findings · Investigations"* for an uncomplicated case |\n| **CT nose and PNS** | *"It is **not essential** to diagnose uncomplicated acute rhinosinusitis"*; it earns its place only *"in assessing for potential complications of acute sinus disease or in anticipation of surgery"* (`enthd-nose-334`) |\n| **Empirical antibiotic therapy** | premature by a week. `L27.1` calls acute bacterial rhinosinusitis *"the **fifth most frequent reason for antibiotic prescription** in the United States"* and devotes a slide to resistance mechanisms |\n\n**Bottom line: at day 3 with no double sickening, the treatment is fluids, analgesia, antipyretics and an explanation.** The same boy at day 10, or after an improvement followed by relapse, becomes `enthd-nose-333`. His follow-up on day 5 is `enthd-nose-318`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'A child three days into a cold with discoloured discharge has viral rhinosinusitis: none of the four bacterial features is present, and purulence is a normal stage of a cold, so the answer is reassurance and symptomatic treatment.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.92. The four bacterial features, the 0.5–2 % conversion rate, the four indications for culture and the CT indication are transcribed from `L27.1) ACUTE rhinosinusitis.pdf`; the watery-then-mucopurulent natural history of the common cold from `L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf`. **⚠️ Chained: `enthd-nose-318` is this same boy on day 5 and restates the case.**' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-318', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-sinusitis',
  stem:'*[The bank prints this as “the same patient” following the previous item; the case is restated so the question stands alone:]* A **4 year old boy** with **bilateral nasal obstruction, discoloured mucoid discharge and fever** since day 3 of a cold **did not improve on follow up on the 5th day but there was no deterioration**. His mother **asks you to prescribe an antibiotic** and mentions that **he gets these colds frequently**. What is the **best next step**?',
  options:['Empirical antibiotic therapy','Influenza vaccine after convalescence','CT nose and paranasal sinuses','Assurance, counselling and watchful waiting'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**Assurance, counselling and watchful waiting — “no improvement” is not “worsening”, and the deck makes the difference between those two words the trigger for an antibiotic.**\n\n`L27.1) ACUTE rhinosinusitis.pdf` writes the fifth-day criterion as ***"Worsening of the symptoms after five days"***, not *failure to improve* by five days. A cold that has plateaued is still doing what a cold does: `L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf` puts *"Resolution within 5-10 days"* on the common-cold slide, so day 5 is the middle of the expected course, not the end of it.\n\n**The mother’s second sentence is answered by the lecture directly, and it is why *counselling* is in the key.** `L27.1` opens with the epidemiology: *"A reasonable estimate is that each adult has two to three colds per year, and **each child has three to eight colds per year**."* Frequent colds in a 4-year-old are **normal**, not a sign of immune deficiency and not an indication to treat. The lecture then says what the consultation is actually for, in its own words:\n\n> *"The misuse of antibiotics should not be a replacement for spending time talking with and examining the patient and **teaching that patient and/or the patient’s family the differences between viral and bacterial infections**."*\n\nThat sentence is the key of this question written out as prose.\n\n| Option | Why it is wrong |\n|---|---|\n| **Empirical antibiotic therapy** | the criterion is worsening, not stalling; and `L27.1` shows what antibiotic-by-request costs — a 10-year-old girl under-dosed on amoxicillin-clavulanate (84 mg of clavulanate where she needed 180 mg) failed, was continued on the same drug, given inadequate ceftriaxone, and ended up **admitted for endoscopic drainage with a culture that grew nothing** |\n| **Influenza vaccine after convalescence** | plausible-sounding preventive care, but this is not influenza and the vaccine does nothing for the rhinovirus and coronavirus colds `L29` names as the commonest; it also does not answer today’s question |\n| **CT nose and paranasal sinuses** | *"not essential to diagnose uncomplicated acute rhinosinusitis"*; radiation to a 4-year-old for a plateaued cold is the wrong side of every trade-off |\n| **Assurance, counselling and watchful waiting** | **keyed** |\n\n**⚠️ WHAT WOULD CHANGE THE ANSWER, and it is worth stating for the parent as a safety-net:** deterioration after day 5, persistence past day 10, symptoms out of proportion, or improvement followed by relapse (**double sickening**) — the four features of `enthd-nose-330` and `entqb-nose4-167`. Any orbital sign — lid swelling, proptosis, painful eye movement — skips all of it and goes straight to imaging and admission (`entep-nose-99`, `entqb-nose4-160`).\n\n**Bottom line: watchful waiting is an active plan with a review date and a list of red flags, not a refusal to treat.** Antecedent case: `enthd-nose-317`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Failure to improve by day 5 is not the same as worsening after day 5; a preschool child has three to eight colds a year normally, so the correct step is reassurance, counselling on viral versus bacterial illness, and watchful waiting with red flags.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.92. **⚠️ Back-references “the same patient” of `enthd-nose-317` (p.92); the case is restated in the stem so the entry stands alone in a shuffled deck.** *"Worsening of the symptoms after five days"*, *"each child has three to eight colds per year"*, the misuse-of-antibiotics slide and the under-dosed 10-year-old case are transcribed from `L27.1) ACUTE rhinosinusitis.pdf`; *"Resolution within 5-10 days"* from `L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf`.' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-319', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-sinusitis',
  stem:'A **22-year-old male** patient comes to your clinic complaining of **severe unilateral headache localized to right aspect of forehead that is exaggerated on bending forwards while doing the prayers**, together with **cough and nasal discharge**. He reports that he had a **common cold infection 2 weeks ago that became worse overtime** till he sought medical consultation. What is the most probable diagnosis?',
  options:['Acute bacterial pansinusitis','Acute bacterial right frontal sinusitis','Chronic rhinosinusitis','Influenza virus infection'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**Acute bacterial right frontal sinusitis — three variables are set by the stem: which sinus, which organism class, and how long.**\n\n**1. Which sinus — the forehead.** Sinus pain is felt over the sinus that is inflamed. The corpus prints the whole map in one item (`entep-nose-72`): maxillary → cheek, **frontal → forehead**, ethmoid → bridge of the nose and between the eyes, sphenoid → occiput. **Right** forehead, **unilateral**, so one frontal sinus and not four sinuses.\n\n**2. Which organism class — the two-week biphasic course.** `L27.1) ACUTE rhinosinusitis.pdf` lists the features that suggest bacterial disease: *"Worsening of the symptoms after five days · Persistence of symptoms for more than 10 days · Presence of symptoms out of proportion to those typically associated with viral URTI · Worsening of symptoms after initial improvement (“double-sickening”)"*. A cold that *"became worse overtime"* over 14 days satisfies the first three at once.\n\n**3. How long — 2 weeks is still acute.** `L27.2) Chronic Sinusitis.pdf` defines chronic rhinosinusitis as *"chronic inflammatory disease of sinus mucosa lasting **more than 12 weeks**"*.\n\n**The posture is the confirmatory detail.** `L32) nasal symptoms 2.pdf` names the frontal pattern outright — *"vacuum headache (morning pain over the forehead due to obstruction of the frontal recess during sleep)"* — and Grade Gain lists *"increase with straining"* among the criteria of sinusitis pain (`entqb-nose4-164`). Bending forward in prostration raises the pressure of retained secretions against the sinus walls.\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Acute bacterial pansinusitis** | *pan-* means all the sinuses, which cannot be **unilateral** and would not localise to one forehead |\n| **Acute bacterial right frontal sinusitis** | **keyed** |\n| **Chronic rhinosinusitis** | needs **≥12 weeks**; this is 2. The trap is reading *2 weeks* as *long-standing* |\n| **Influenza virus infection** | `L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf` puts influenza among the **acute viral** rhinosinusitides — abrupt, systemic, self-limiting, and it does not produce two weeks of progressive unilateral frontal pain |\n\n**⚠️ A frontal sinusitis that has been progressing for two weeks is the one to take seriously**, because the frontal sinus is the source of both `entqb-nose4-163` (Pott’s puffy tumour, through the anterior table) and `entep-nose-11` / `entqb-nose4-161` (frontal lobe abscess, through the posterior table). Ask about forehead swelling and about personality change.\n\n**Bottom line: the site of the pain names the sinus, the course names the organism, and twelve weeks — not two — is what makes a sinusitis chronic.** The investigation of this same patient is `enthd-nose-320`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Unilateral frontal headache worse on bending forward, two weeks after a cold that kept worsening, is acute bacterial frontal sinusitis on that side — pansinusitis cannot be unilateral and chronic rhinosinusitis needs twelve weeks.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.92. The four bacterial features are transcribed from `L27.1) ACUTE rhinosinusitis.pdf`, the 12-week definition from `L27.2) Chronic Sinusitis.pdf`, *"vacuum headache"* from `L32) nasal symptoms 2.pdf`, and influenza as an acute viral rhinosinusitis from `L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf`. The sinus-to-site pain map is taken from the corpus item `entep-nose-72` in preference to outside knowledge. **⚠️ Chained: `enthd-nose-320` asks the investigation for this case.**' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-320', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-sinusitis',
  stem:'*[The bank prints this as “the previous case”; it is restated so the question stands alone:]* A **22-year-old male** with a **severe unilateral right frontal headache exaggerated on bending forwards**, cough and nasal discharge, **2 weeks after a common cold that kept worsening**, has **acute bacterial right frontal sinusitis**. What is the **most appropriate means of diagnosis**?',
  options:['CT nose and paranasal sinuses','MRI nose and paranasal sinuses','X ray nose and paranasal sinuses','Clinical diagnosis'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**Clinical diagnosis — acute rhinosinusitis is diagnosed at the bedside, and the deck says so in as many words.**\n\n`L27.1) ACUTE rhinosinusitis.pdf` heads a slide *"How to Diagnose ABRS"* and prints three boxes: ***History · Clinical findings · Investigations***, in that order. The clinical criterion follows on the next slide: *"In a patient with **two or more major** signs or symptoms, **one major and two minor**, or **greater than three minor** signs or symptoms, the diagnosis of acute rhinosinusitis is **strongly suspected**."* This patient has facial pain localised over one sinus, nasal discharge and cough, and a biphasic course — the criteria are met before any machine is switched on.\n\nThe lecture then removes the imaging options one at a time:\n\n| Option | The deck’s own position |\n|---|---|\n| **CT nose and PNS** | *"It is **not essential** to diagnose uncomplicated acute rhinosinusitis. It is certainly an important diagnostic tool in assessing for **potential complications** of acute sinus disease or **in anticipation of surgery** on the sinuses."* Neither applies here — no orbital sign, no neurological sign, no operation planned (`enthd-nose-334`) |\n| **MRI nose and PNS** | `L27.2) Chronic Sinusitis.pdf` is blunt: *"MRI for diagnosis is **discouraged** because of increased cost and (overdiagnosis) in comparison to CT without contrast."* If MRI is second to CT even in chronic disease, it cannot be first in acute disease |\n| **X ray nose and PNS** | plain films are not named anywhere in the deck’s diagnostic pathway. Air–fluid levels and mucosal thickening are neither sensitive nor specific, and they cannot separate viral from bacterial mucosa — the very distinction the whole lecture is built around. Even for chronic disease the corpus keys CT over plain X-ray (`entep-nose-23`) |\n| **Clinical diagnosis** | **keyed** |\n\n**⚠️ THE CONTRAST WITH CHRONIC DISEASE IS THE POINT WORTH CARRYING AWAY.** For **chronic** rhinosinusitis imaging is not optional — Grade Gain prints *"investigations is important in chronic rhinosinusitis (**mandatory for diagnosis**)"* (`entqb-nose4-158`), because `L27.2` requires inflammation to be **documented** by endoscopy or *"radiographic imaging showing inflammation of the paranasal sinuses"*. **Acute: clinical. Chronic: clinical plus CT.** The plane and contrast for that CT are `enthd-nose-323`.\n\n**The examination that does add something here is not radiological**: `L27.1` illustrates the endoscopic tour and what it looks for — *"congestion and mucopurulent discharge"*, *"pus in the middle meatus"*, *"pus in the sphenoethmoidal recess"*.\n\n**Bottom line: image an acute sinusitis when you suspect a complication or plan surgery, not to make the diagnosis.** Antecedent case: `enthd-nose-319`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Uncomplicated acute rhinosinusitis is a clinical diagnosis by the major/minor symptom criteria; CT is reserved for suspected complications or preoperative planning, MRI is discouraged, and plain X-ray has no role.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.93. **⚠️ Back-references “the previous case” = `enthd-nose-319` (p.92); the case is restated in the stem so the entry stands alone.** The diagnostic triad, the major/minor criterion, the endoscopic findings and the CT indication are transcribed from `L27.1) ACUTE rhinosinusitis.pdf`; the MRI position and the documentation requirement from `L27.2) Chronic Sinusitis.pdf`.' }
,

{ id:'enthd-nose-321', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-sinuscomp',
  stem:'Which of the following is **true regarding sinusitis complications**?',
  options:['Sinusitis is the most common cause of frontal lobe abscess','Sinusitis complications are more common in chronic cases than acute ones','Meningitis is among the cranial complications of sinusitis','Sinusitis complications can be managed without hospitalization'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**Sinusitis is the most common cause of frontal lobe abscess — and this is not outside reasoning, it is printed by the course’s own second bank.**\n\n**⚠️ NO CACHED LECTURE COVERS THE COMPLICATIONS OF RHINOSINUSITIS.** The supplied slide set runs `L27.2) Chronic Sinusitis` straight to `L29) Rhinitis 1` with no L28, and greps for *orbital cellulitis*, *Pott* and *frontal lobe* return nothing in either rhinosinusitis deck. **The gap is therefore filled from the printed banks in preference to outside knowledge.** Grade Gain prints the exact sentence in its own explanation box (`entqb-nose4-162`):\n\n> *"C. is not correct as **sinusitis is the commonest cause of frontal lobe abscess**."*\n\nThe anatomy behind it: only a thin **posterior table** of the frontal bone separates the frontal sinus from the frontal lobe, and the **diploic veins of the frontal bone are valveless**, so infection travels by direct erosion or retrograde thrombophlebitis without crossing any barrier that matters *(the venous detail is not taken from the course material)*. The corpus prints the clinical picture twice — headache with **personality change** after a sinusitis (`entep-nose-11`, `entqb-nose4-161`).\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **Sinusitis is the most common cause of frontal lobe abscess** | **keyed** — printed verbatim by Grade Gain |\n| **More common in chronic than acute cases** | **false, and it is the mistake this fact exists to correct.** The same Grade Gain box says *"complications occur in **acute and chronic** rhinosinusitis. But **more on top of acute** rhinosinusitis."* A one-week cold in a healthy child can produce an orbital abscess — see `enthd-nose-336`, which keys exactly this |\n| **Meningitis is among the **cranial** complications** | **the classification is wrong, not the fact.** Meningitis is a genuine complication, but the ENT taxonomy the course teaches puts it under **intracranial**: `L16) Chronic otitis media.pdf` prints *"Cranial complications: Mastoiditis · Labyrinthitis · Petrositis · Facial paralysis"* against *"**Intracranial** complications: **Meningitis** · Extradural abscess · Subdural abscess · Brain abscess"*. **The word *cranial* is what makes the statement false.** Read this option slowly — it is true in substance and false as written |\n| **Can be managed without hospitalization** | **false.** A collection is drained: intravenous antibiotics, imaging and surgical drainage of both the abscess and the offending sinus. Grade Gain rejects the same distractor written as *"usually managed medically"* (`entqb-nose4-162`); only pre-septal cellulitis is an outpatient problem |\n\n**Bottom line: sinus abscesses go FORWARD to the frontal lobe; ear abscesses go to the temporal lobe and cerebellum** (`L16)`); **and the dangerous sinusitis is the acute one, in a patient with no sinus history at all.**\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Sinusitis is the commonest cause of frontal lobe abscess; complications arise more on top of acute than chronic disease, meningitis is an INTRAcranial rather than cranial complication, and any collection needs admission and drainage.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.93. **⚠️ NOT COVERED BY ANY CACHED LECTURE** — the ENT set has no complications-of-rhinosinusitis file (no L28). The keyed sentence and the acute-versus-chronic correction are taken **verbatim from Grade Gain’s printed box at `entqb-nose4-162`** in preference to outside knowledge; the cranial/intracranial/extracranial taxonomy is transcribed from `L16) Chronic otitis media.pdf`. The valveless diploic veins are tagged in the explanation as outside the course material. Cross-references: `entep-nose-11`, `entqb-nose4-161`, `entqb-nose4-163`, `enthd-nose-325`, `enthd-nose-336`.' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-322', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-noseanat',
  stem:'All of the following sinuses open into **ostiomeatal complex** **except**?',
  options:['Frontal sinus','Maxillary sinus','Anterior ethmoid sinus','Posterior ethmoid sinus'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The posterior ethmoid sinus — it drains into the SUPERIOR meatus, and the ostiomeatal complex is entirely a structure of the MIDDLE meatus.**\n\n`L23) Anatomy of the Nose.pdf` prints the drainage map that decides every option, on one slide headed *"The Lateral wall (3 turbinates & 3 meati)"*:\n\n| Meatus / recess | What drains there (the deck’s own words) |\n|---|---|\n| **Inferior meatus** | *"NLD opens in the inferior meatus"* — the nasolacrimal duct, no sinus at all |\n| **Middle meatus** | *"**Frontal, anterior ethmoids & maxillary sinuses** opens in the middle meatus"* — the **anterior group**, and this is the ostiomeatal complex |\n| **Superior meatus** | *"**Posterio ethmoids** opens in the superior meatus"* (transcribed with the deck’s spelling of *posterior*) |\n| **Sphenoethmoidal recess** | *"Sphenoid sinus opens in the sphenoethmoidal recess"* |\n\nSo the sinuses split into a **front three** and a **back two**, and the ostiomeatal complex is the shared final common channel of the front three. Its components are set out in `enthd-nose-237` and are not repeated here.\n\n| Option | Drains into | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Frontal sinus** | middle meatus, via the frontal recess | member of the complex |\n| **Maxillary sinus** | middle meatus, via the ethmoid infundibulum and hiatus semilunaris | member |\n| **Anterior ethmoid sinus** | middle meatus, around the bulla ethmoidalis | member |\n| **Posterior ethmoid sinus** | **superior meatus** | **keyed — the exception** |\n\n**⚠️ WHY THIS ANATOMY IS THE WHOLE OF SINUS SURGERY.** Because three sinuses share one narrow channel, **a single block in the ostiomeatal complex infects all three at once** — which is why `L27.2) Chronic Sinusitis.pdf` lists *"anatomical variations"* among the triggers of chronic rhinosinusitis, why the CT is read in the **coronal** plane where the complex is displayed (`enthd-nose-323`, `enthd-nose-324`), and why the operation is called ***functional*** endoscopic sinus surgery: it opens the channel rather than stripping the sinuses.\n\n**A useful corollary for the endoscopy question:** pus seen **in the middle meatus** means the anterior group; pus **in the sphenoethmoidal recess** means the posterior group. `L27.1) ACUTE rhinosinusitis.pdf` prints exactly those two findings side by side on its endoscopy slide.\n\n**Bottom line: front three to the middle meatus, posterior ethmoid to the superior meatus, sphenoid to the sphenoethmoidal recess, tears to the inferior meatus.**\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'The ostiomeatal complex is the shared drainage channel of the anterior sinus group at the middle meatus — frontal, anterior ethmoid and maxillary; the posterior ethmoid drains into the superior meatus and the sphenoid into the sphenoethmoidal recess.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.93. The four-row drainage map is transcribed from `L23) Anatomy of the Nose.pdf` (the deck spells *"Posterio ethmoids"*, kept as printed); *"anatomical variations"* from `L27.2) Chronic Sinusitis.pdf`; the two endoscopic pus sites from `L27.1) ACUTE rhinosinusitis.pdf`. Straddles `ent-sinusitis`; filed to `ent-noseanat` because the question is pure drainage anatomy. Cross-reference: `enthd-nose-237` carries the component list of the complex.' }
,

{ id:'enthd-nose-323', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-sinusitis',
  stem:'What is the **most appropriate investigation** to be done in a case of **chronic rhinosinusitis**?',
  options:['Contrast-enhanced CT nose and paranasal sinuses, axial view','Non-contrast CT nose and paranasal sinuses, axial view','Contrast-enhanced CT nose and paranasal sinuses, coronal view','Non-contrast CT nose and paranasal sinuses, coronal view'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**Non-contrast CT in the coronal plane — the menu varies two things at once, so the answer needs both decided separately.**\n\n**Decision 1 — CT, and without contrast.** `L27.2) Chronic Sinusitis.pdf` settles the modality by ruling out its rival, and it names the comparator in the same breath: *"**MRI for diagnosis is discouraged** because of increased cost and (overdiagnosis) **in comparison to CT without contrast**."* The words *CT without contrast* are the deck’s own description of the standard study. The corpus agrees twice over — `entep-nose-23` keys CT over MRI, ultrasound and plain X-ray, and Grade Gain prints *"investigations is important in chronic rhinosinusitis (**mandatory for diagnosis**)"* (`entqb-nose4-158`). Contrast adds nothing to what CT is being asked here, which is **bone and air**: the mucosa, the ostia and the bony walls are all high-contrast on plain CT already.\n\n**Decision 2 — coronal, because that is the plane in which the ostiomeatal complex exists.** The complex is a vertical channel running down the lateral wall of the middle meatus, so a **coronal** slice cuts across the uncinate process, the ethmoid infundibulum, the bulla ethmoidalis, the middle turbinate and the maxillary ostium **all on one image**, and displays the lamina papyracea, the orbital floor and the skull base above them. The corpus states this from the other direction while arguing for the axial plane in choanal atresia: coronal *"is the right plane for the **osteomeatal complex** and for **sinusitis**; it is the wrong one here"* (`entqb-nose1-38`). Grade Gain’s atrophic-rhinitis key likewise prints a **coronal** CT (`entqb-nose3-104`).\n\n| Option | Which half is wrong |\n|---|---|\n| **Contrast, axial** | both |\n| **Non-contrast, axial** | plane — axial is for the choana and for the anteroposterior extent of a mass, not for the drainage channel |\n| **Contrast, coronal** | contrast — an unnecessary iodine load with no gain in a straightforward chronic case |\n| **Non-contrast, coronal** | **keyed** |\n\n**⚠️ WHEN CONTRAST DOES EARN ITS PLACE, so that the rule is not over-learned:** when the question is no longer *is this sinusitis* but *is this something else or has it escaped the sinus* — the suspicion of **neoplasm**, of an **orbital or intracranial complication**, or of **invasive fungal disease**. `L27.2` frames precisely that role: *"An important role of CT imaging in CRS with or without polyps is to **exclude aggressive infections or neoplastic disease** that might mimic CRS or ARS"* *(the specific decision to add contrast for those indications is not taken from the course material)*.\n\n**Bottom line: plain coronal CT is the map the sinus surgeon operates from — it shows the ostiomeatal complex, the skull base and the lamina papyracea in one plane.** What that map is used for is `enthd-nose-324`; when NOT to order it is `enthd-nose-334`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Chronic rhinosinusitis is imaged by non-contrast CT in the coronal plane — the plane that displays the ostiomeatal complex, skull base and lamina papyracea together; MRI is discouraged and contrast is reserved for suspected neoplasm or complications.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.93. *"MRI for diagnosis is discouraged … in comparison to CT without contrast"* and the exclusion of aggressive infection or neoplasia are transcribed from `L27.2) Chronic Sinusitis.pdf`. **The choice of the coronal plane is printed by no cached deck; it is taken from Grade Gain `entqb-nose1-38` and the coronal key image of `entqb-nose3-104` in preference to outside knowledge.** The contrast indications are tagged in the explanation as outside the course material. Cross-references: `entep-nose-23`, `entqb-nose4-158`.' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-324', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-sinusitis',
  stem:'**CT scan of paranasal sinuses in case of chronic sinusitis** is required to …?',
  options:['Locate the diseased sinuses','Define ostiomeatal complex abnormalities','Diagnose DNS','All of the above','None of the above'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**All of the above — the CT is not a diagnostic test in chronic rhinosinusitis so much as a surgical map, and each option names one thing the map has to carry.**\n\nThe diagnosis itself is already made clinically before the scan: `L27.2) Chronic Sinusitis.pdf` requires *"**Twelve weeks or longer** of two or more of … mucopurulent drainage · nasal obstruction (congestion) · facial pain/pressure/fullness · decreased sense of smell"*, with inflammation documented by purulent mucus or oedema in the middle meatus, polyps, **or** *"radiographic imaging showing inflammation of the paranasal sinuses"*. What the CT adds is everything the surgeon needs afterwards.\n\n| Option | What the scan gives, and why it matters |\n|---|---|\n| **Locate the diseased sinuses** | `L27.2` says *"**one sinus or several sinuses** can be involved"*, so the extent has to be established rather than assumed; the documentation clause above is this option written as a criterion |\n| **Define ostiomeatal complex abnormalities** | the reason the plane is **coronal** (`enthd-nose-323`). `L27.2` lists *"anatomical variations"* among the triggers of chronic rhinosinusitis, and the complex is the shared channel of the anterior group (`enthd-nose-322`) — clearing it is what the word ***functional*** means in functional endoscopic sinus surgery |\n| **Diagnose DNS** | a deviated septum both **contributes** to obstruction of the complex and **blocks the endoscope’s access** to it; the septum has to be assessed before an operation is planned, and is often corrected in the same sitting *(the operative point is not taken from the course material)* |\n| **All of the above** | **keyed** |\n| **None of the above** | refuted by any single row |\n\n**⚠️ THE ITEM THE OPTION LIST DOES NOT NAME IS THE ONE THAT CAUSES HARM WHEN IT IS MISSED**, and it is worth adding to the reading routine *(not taken from the course material)*: the **danger anatomy** — the height and asymmetry of the ethmoid roof and the depth of the olfactory fossa, the position of the anterior ethmoidal artery, dehiscence of the lamina papyracea, and the relation of the optic nerve and carotid artery to a pneumatised posterior ethmoid cell. `L27.2` also gives the scan a **diagnostic** duty beyond drainage: *"an important role of CT imaging in CRS with or without polyps is to **exclude aggressive infections or neoplastic disease** that might mimic CRS or ARS."*\n\n**Bottom line: in chronic rhinosinusitis the CT answers *where*, *why it will not drain*, and *what is in the way* — not *whether*.** The threshold for surgery itself is medical failure: `L27.2` requires *"at least 12 weeks of nasal saline, INCS, and often systemic steroids"* first (`entep-nose-17`, `entqb-nose4-159`).\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'In chronic rhinosinusitis the CT is a surgical map: it locates the diseased sinuses, defines ostiomeatal complex abnormalities and shows septal deviation — the diagnosis itself is already clinical.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.94. The 12-week symptom criteria, the documentation clause, *"one sinus or several sinuses can be involved"*, *"anatomical variations"*, the exclusion of neoplasia and the pre-surgical medical requirement are all transcribed from `L27.2) Chronic Sinusitis.pdf`. The septoplasty-for-access point and the danger-anatomy checklist are tagged in the explanation as outside the course material. Cross-references: `enthd-nose-322`, `enthd-nose-323`, `entqb-nose4-158`.' }
,

{ id:'enthd-nose-325', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-sinuscomp',
  stem:'**T / F:** Complications of rhinosinusitis occur **more frequently in adults than in children**, especially **orbital** complications.',
  options:['True','False'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**False — it is the other way round, and the orbital complications in particular are a paediatric problem.**\n\n**⚠️ A TRUE/FALSE MENU CARRIES NO CONTENT, so this entry is grounded entirely in what the sources print — and the cached decks print nothing on this topic.** The ENT slide set runs `L27.2) Chronic Sinusitis` straight to `L29) Rhinitis 1`; *orbital cellulitis*, *Pott* and *cavernous* all return zero in both rhinosinusitis decks. **The statement is therefore adjudicated from the two printed banks rather than from outside knowledge**, and both say the same thing in their own explanation boxes:\n\n> Grade Gain, `entqb-nose4-160`: *"Orbital complications are more serious. **More in children due to weak ossification**"*, and *"Child with eye swelling during respiratory tract infection = complicated rhinosinusitis"*.\n>\n> Endpoint, `entep-nose-34` (the p.1011 box): *"**Orbital complications of rhinosinusitis are more frequent in children than in adults** because of the thin lamina papyracea, which separates the orbit from the ethmoid sinuses."*\n\n**Why children — three reasons, and the first is the one the boxes give.**\n\n| | In a child | Consequence |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Lamina papyracea** | thin and **incompletely ossified** (*"weak ossification"*), often dehiscent | infection crosses from ethmoid to orbit directly |\n| **Which sinus is developed** | the **ethmoid is pneumatised from birth**; the frontal sinus barely exists until adolescence | the sinus that is available to infect is the one lying against the orbit *(not taken from the course material)* |\n| **Valveless veins** | run freely between the ethmoid mucosa and the orbit | septic thrombophlebitis without bone erosion *(not taken from the course material)* |\n\n**The corpus keys this three separate ways, which is how much weight the examiners put on it:** `entep-nose-34` — the **ethmoid** is the sinus that causes orbital complications; `entep-nose-104` — **orbital cellulitis** is the commonest complication of acute ethmoid sinusitis in a child; `entqb-nose4-160` — a **7-year-old with a swollen eye after a cold** is complicated rhinosinusitis until proved otherwise. `entep-nose-99` gives the answer to *what next*: **CT of the nose and paranasal sinuses**, urgently.\n\n**⚠️ THE SIGNS THAT SEPARATE PRE-SEPTAL FROM POST-SEPTAL ARE WHAT THE EXAMINER WANTS AT THE BEDSIDE** *(the septum-based classification is not taken from the course material)*: lid swelling and redness occur in both; **proptosis, painful or restricted eye movement, diplopia, reduced vision or colour desaturation** mean the infection is inside the orbit and the child is admitted for intravenous antibiotics, urgent CT and drainage of any collection.\n\n**Bottom line: a child’s eye that swells during a cold is a sinus emergency; an adult’s is much less often one.** Read the statement’s two halves separately — both are false here, and the word *especially* does not rescue it.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Complications of rhinosinusitis, and orbital complications above all, are commoner in CHILDREN than adults — the lamina papyracea is thin and incompletely ossified and the ethmoid is the only well-developed sinus.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.94 (printed as a True/False item). **⚠️ NOT COVERED BY ANY CACHED LECTURE** — the ENT set has no complications-of-rhinosinusitis file (no L28), and a True/False menu carries no content of its own. Adjudicated from the printed explanation boxes of Grade Gain `entqb-nose4-160` and endpoint `entep-nose-34` in preference to outside knowledge; the ethmoid’s pneumatisation at birth, the valveless veins and the pre-septal/post-septal signs are tagged in the explanation as outside the course material. Cross-references: `entep-nose-104`, `entep-nose-99`, `enthd-nose-321`, `enthd-nose-336`.' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-326', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-sinusitis',
  stem:'Which of the following is the **most common dental origin of maxillary sinusitis**?',
  options:['Upper central incisors','Upper 2nd premolar and 1st molar teeth','Upper 1st premolar and 2nd molar teeth','Lower lateral incisor tooth'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The upper second premolar and first molar — their roots are the ones that sit against the floor of the maxillary antrum, separated from it by a wafer of bone or by mucosa alone.**\n\n**⚠️ The cached decks name the mechanism but not the teeth.** `L27.2) Chronic Sinusitis.pdf` lists *"Infection of an adjacent organ (**e.g. teeth**)"* among the triggers, and `L31) nasal symptoms 1.pdf` files *"Odontogenic sinusitis"* under foetid nasal discharge and under *"unilateral pathology"* — but no slide names a tooth. **The specific pair is printed by the course’s own second bank and is taken from there in preference to outside knowledge.** Endpoint prints it in an explanation box (`entep-nose-24`, also printed by Grade Gain):\n\n> *"The roots of the **second premolar and first molar** are closest to the **maxillary sinus floor**, predisposing them to sinus complications with dental infections. Incisors, canines, and wisdom teeth are less intimately related."*\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Upper central incisors** | far forward, and their roots point at the **floor of the nose**, not the antrum — the maxillary sinus does not extend that anteriorly |\n| **Upper 2nd premolar and 1st molar** | **keyed** |\n| **Upper 1st premolar and 2nd molar** | **the trap, and it is one digit away from the key on both halves.** The list is *second* premolar and *first* molar; this option swaps the ordinals. Read the numbers before the tooth names |\n| **Lower lateral incisor tooth** | a **lower** tooth cannot reach the maxilla at all — its infections track to the submandibular and submental spaces and to Ludwig’s angina (`entqb-thr3-93`) |\n\n**Two clinical consequences follow from the same anatomy, and both are asked elsewhere.** The infection can travel **tooth → sinus**, giving the unilateral foetid discharge of odontogenic sinusitis (`enthd-nose-335`, `entep-nose-54`); and the **extraction** can open the antrum, giving an **oro-antral fistula** — endpoint keys the upper second molar as its commonest cause (`entep-nose-102`). It also runs in the reverse direction: antral inflammation is felt in the upper teeth through the **superior alveolar nerves** in the sinus walls, which is why the vignette of `entep-nose-85` presents as *"upper jaw pain"*.\n\n**⚠️ A single-tooth version of this question exists and its key looks different but is not**: `entep-nose-62` asks for **the** tooth most intimately related and keys the **upper first molar** — which is in this pair. The two keys are consistent.\n\n**Bottom line: second premolar, first molar, upper — one side, one antrum, one tooth to look for.**\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'The roots of the upper second premolar and first molar lie against the maxillary sinus floor, which is why odontogenic maxillary sinusitis and oro-antral fistula come from those teeth.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.94. *"Infection of an adjacent organ (e.g. teeth)"* is transcribed from `L27.2) Chronic Sinusitis.pdf` and the odontogenic entries from `L31) nasal symptoms 1.pdf`. **No cached deck names the teeth; the pair is taken verbatim from endpoint’s printed box at `entep-nose-24` (also printed by Grade Gain) in preference to outside knowledge.** Cross-references: `entep-nose-62`, `entep-nose-102`, `entep-nose-85`, `enthd-nose-327`, `enthd-nose-328`, `enthd-nose-335`.' }
,

{ id:'enthd-nose-327', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-sinusitis',
  stem:'Which of the following is a **predisposing factor for recurrent unilateral maxillary sinusitis** that occurs **over and over in the same side**?',
  options:['DM','Allergic rhinitis','Dental sepsis','AIDS'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**Dental sepsis — the words that decide this are *unilateral* and *the same side*, and only one of the four options is confined to one side of the face.**\n\n`L31) nasal symptoms 1.pdf` states the principle the question is built on: *"**Unilateral** nasal obstruction usually points to structural abnormality or **uni-lateral pathology** (antrochoanal polyp, **odontogenic pathology**)"*, while *"**Bilateral** nasal obstruction may be mucosal, structural, or mixed"*. The same deck files *"Odontogenic sinusitis"* under **foetid** nasal discharge. `L27.2) Chronic Sinusitis.pdf` supplies the mechanism among its triggers: *"Infection of an adjacent organ (**e.g. teeth**)"*.\n\n**One diseased tooth seeds one antrum.** The roots of the upper second premolar and first molar sit against that sinus floor (`enthd-nose-326`, `entep-nose-24`), so until the tooth is treated the same antrum reinfects — which is exactly the *over and over in the same side* pattern the stem describes.\n\n| Option | Why it cannot explain a fixed side |\n|---|---|\n| **DM** | a systemic immune deficit — it predisposes to sinus infection in general, and to **acute invasive fungal sinusitis** in particular (`L30) Rhinitis 2.pdf` names *"poorly controlled diabetics"* as one of its two populations), but it has no laterality |\n| **Allergic rhinitis** | a genuine predisposing factor — `L27.1) ACUTE rhinosinusitis.pdf` lists **allergy** among its predisposing factors and explains it (*"expression of the inflammatory adhesion molecule, ICAM-1, is elevated in patients with AR responsible for increased susceptibility to respiratory infections"*) — but `L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf` defines it as a hypersensitivity of the **nasal mucous membrane** as a whole, so it acts on both sides |\n| **Dental sepsis** | **keyed** |\n| **AIDS** | immunosuppression, again bilateral; `L27.1` lists it only as one of the four settings in which a **culture** is justified |\n\n**⚠️ THE RULE THIS QUESTION IS A DISGUISED VERSION OF:** *unilateral offensive discharge in an adult is odontogenic sinusitis until proved otherwise; the same complaint in a child is a foreign body* — the endpoint box quoted at `entep-nose-54`. **And the third possibility must not be forgotten**: a recurrent, one-sided, blood-stained or foetid sinusitis in an older adult that will not settle is also how a **maxillary malignancy** presents (`entep-nose-30`), which is why persistent unilateral disease is scoped and imaged rather than re-treated.\n\n**Bottom line: a sinusitis that keeps choosing the same side has a local cause on that side — look at the teeth first, then at the nose.** Treatment addresses both compartments: antibiotics **with dental referral** (`entqb-nose4-168`).\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Recurrent maxillary sinusitis that always returns on the same side has a local unilateral cause — dental sepsis; diabetes, allergy and AIDS predispose systemically and cannot fix a side.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.94. The unilateral-versus-bilateral rule and *"Odontogenic sinusitis"* are transcribed from `L31) nasal symptoms 1.pdf`; *"Infection of an adjacent organ (e.g. teeth)"* from `L27.2) Chronic Sinusitis.pdf`; allergy, ICAM-1 and the culture indications from `L27.1) ACUTE rhinosinusitis.pdf`; *"poorly controlled diabetics"* from `L30) Rhinitis 2.pdf`; the definition of allergic rhinitis from `L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf`. Cross-references: `entep-nose-54`, `entep-nose-30`, `entqb-nose4-168`, `enthd-nose-326`, `enthd-nose-335`.' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-328', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-sinusitis',
  stem:'All of the following are characteristics of **acute odontogenic rhinosinusitis** (rhinosinusitis originating from dental infection) **except**?',
  options:['It causes bilateral foetid nasal discharge','It’s responsible for like 10% of cases of acute bacterial rhinosinusitis','It mainly carries the infection to maxillary sinus','It mostly originates from upper 2nd premolar and 1st molar teeth'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The exception is *bilateral* — the discharge of odontogenic sinusitis is foetid, and that half is right, but it is UNILATERAL, because one tooth infects one antrum.**\n\n`L31) nasal symptoms 1.pdf` prints both halves of the sign from opposite directions. Under *"Nasal Discharge: Foetid"* it lists *"1. Foreign bodies in the nose · 2. **Odontogenic sinusitis** · 3. Sinonasal neoplasms · 4. Fungal infections"*; and under laterality it teaches that unilateral disease *"usually points to structural abnormality or uni-lateral pathology (antrochoanal polyp, **odontogenic pathology**)"*. `L32) nasal symptoms 2.pdf` closes the loop with a True/False item of its own — *"Odontogenic sinusitis is characterized by **watery** discharge"* — printed to be answered **false**.\n\nThe corpus keys the two attributes together in one option: *"**Unilateral and foetid**"* (`entep-nose-54`), whose printed box reads *"unilateral offensive fetid discharge in adult = sinusitis of dental origin till proved otherwise ▪ if the same complain from a child = foreign body."* **Foetid because the organisms are oral anaerobes rather than the respiratory pathogens of ordinary rhinosinusitis; unilateral because the source is one tooth.**\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **Bilateral foetid discharge** | **keyed as the exception** — foetid yes, bilateral no |\n| **Responsible for like 10% of cases** | true as printed. **⚠️ No cached deck gives any proportion**; roughly one in ten of acute bacterial maxillary sinusitis being odontogenic is the classically quoted figure, and modern endoscopic series put it considerably higher, at a quarter to two-fifths *(not taken from the course material)* |\n| **Mainly carries the infection to the maxillary sinus** | true — it is the sinus whose **floor** is the dental alveolus; no other sinus abuts a tooth |\n| **Mostly from upper 2nd premolar and 1st molar** | true — endpoint’s box: *"the roots of the second premolar and first molar are closest to the maxillary sinus floor"* (`entep-nose-24`, `enthd-nose-326`) |\n\n**⚠️ THE AGE CLAUSE IS THE HALF STUDENTS DROP.** The same sign — one-sided, offensive discharge — means **dental sepsis in an adult** and a **nasal foreign body in a child** (`entep-nose-18`). In an older adult who does not settle, add **maxillary malignancy** to the list (`entep-nose-30`).\n\n**Bottom line: unilateral + offensive + an adult + a recent extraction, root canal or bad tooth = odontogenic maxillary sinusitis**, treated with broad-spectrum cover **and** a dental opinion, because antibiotics alone leave the source in place (`entqb-nose4-168`, `entep-mfe4-29`).\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Odontogenic rhinosinusitis is unilateral and foetid, reaches the maxillary sinus, and arises from the upper second premolar and first molar — the bilateral claim is the exception.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.94. The foetid-discharge list and the laterality rule are transcribed from `L31) nasal symptoms 1.pdf`; the *"watery discharge"* True/False item from `L32) nasal symptoms 2.pdf`; the unilateral-offensive rule and the dental anatomy from endpoint’s printed boxes at `entep-nose-54` and `entep-nose-24`. **⚠️ The 10 % proportion is printed by no cached deck and by no other bank; it is transcribed as the option prints it and the modern range is tagged in the explanation as outside the course material.** Cross-references: `entep-nose-18`, `entep-nose-30`, `entqb-nose4-168`, `entep-mfe4-29`.' }
,

{ id:'enthd-nose-329', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-sinusitis',
  stem:'Which of the following is the **most common cause for acute sinusitis**?',
  options:['Allergic rhinitis','Nasal tumors','Deviated nasal septum','Common cold'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The common cold — a viral upper respiratory infection is where essentially every acute sinusitis begins, and the deck opens its pathophysiology slide with exactly that word.**\n\n`L27.1) ACUTE rhinosinusitis.pdf` heads *"Pathophysiology of ABRS"* with a single box reading ***"Upper Respiratory tract infection"***, and quantifies it on the burden slide: *"each adult has **two to three colds per year**, and each child has **three to eight colds per year**. **Up to 80% of these URTIs may be associated with rhinosinusitis**, equating to over one billion cases annually in the United States."*\n\nThe reason is in the definition itself, on the deck’s first slide: *"The nasal mucosa is **contiguous** with that of the paranasal sinuses; **any inflammation of the sinuses is almost always accompanied by inflammation of the nasal cavity**."* `L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf` says it from the other end — its **complications of the common cold** list begins *"**Sinusitis** · Pharyngitis · Adenoiditis · Tonsillitis · Otitis media…"* — and names the organisms: *"the commonest viral infection in human … **Rhinovirus · Coronavirus**."*\n\n| Option | Its actual status |\n|---|---|\n| **Allergic rhinitis** | a **predisposing factor**, not the cause. `L27.1` lists *"Allergy"* second in its nine predisposing factors and explains why — *"ICAM-1 is elevated in patients with AR, responsible for increased susceptibility to respiratory infections"*. It makes the cold likelier to sinus; it is not what infects the sinus |\n| **Nasal tumors** | rare, **unilateral**, and a cause of **obstruction** rather than of acute infection; `L26) nasal masses.pdf` flags persistent unilateral obstruction, rhinorrhoea, epistaxis and sinus pain as alarm features precisely because they are *not* the ordinary pattern |\n| **Deviated nasal septum** | again predisposing — one of the *"anatomical variations"* `L27.1` and `L27.2` both list, and a reason a CT is read before surgery (`enthd-nose-324`) |\n| **Common cold** | **keyed** |\n\n**The distinction the option list is drilling is *cause* versus *predisposition*, and the last two options are the deck’s own predisposing-factor list used as distractors** — *"environmental exposure · allergy · anatomical variations · smoking · ciliary impairment · laryngopharyngeal reflux · anxiety and depression · drug resistance · concomitant chronic disease"*.\n\n**⚠️ AND THE COROLLARY THAT MATTERS MOST CLINICALLY:** if the cold causes it, then **most acute rhinosinusitis is viral and self-limiting** — only *"0.5% to 2.0%"* of that billion becomes bacterial (`enthd-nose-317`, `enthd-nose-331`).\n\n**Bottom line: cold → mucosal oedema → ostial obstruction → stagnation in the sinus.** ⚠️ *Not to be confused with `entqb-nose5-190`, which also keys the common cold but asks the commonest cause of **nasal obstruction** — a different question with the same answer.*\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Acute rhinosinusitis follows the common cold: up to 80 % of URTIs involve the sinuses because nasal and sinus mucosa are contiguous. Allergy, septal deviation and tumours predispose or obstruct; they do not cause it.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.95. The pathophysiology box, the 80 % figure, the cold frequencies, the contiguity statement, the nine predisposing factors and the 0.5–2 % conversion rate are transcribed from `L27.1) ACUTE rhinosinusitis.pdf`; the complications-of-the-common-cold list and the viral agents from `L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf`; the unilateral alarm list from `L26) nasal masses.pdf`. ⚠️ **Not a duplicate of `entqb-nose5-190`** — recorded in `house-sweep-nose.md` as a shared-key false positive: same key, different question (commonest cause of *nasal obstruction*).' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-330', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-sinusitis',
  stem:'Which of the following is a **characteristic of acute rhinosinusitis**?',
  options:['Never lasts for more than 10 days','Double sickening within 4-5 days of common cold infection','The majority of cases turn into chronic cases','All of the above'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**Double sickening — improvement followed by abrupt worsening — and the deck prints it as one of exactly four features that mark the turn from viral to bacterial disease.**\n\n`L27.1) ACUTE rhinosinusitis.pdf`, under *"It is important to distinguish between viral and bacterial rhinosinusitis"*:\n\n> *"Factors suggesting acute bacterial sinusitis are: • **Worsening of the symptoms after five days.** • **Persistence of symptoms for more than 10 days.** • **Presence of symptoms out of proportion** to those typically associated with viral URTI. • **Worsening of symptoms after initial improvement (“double-sickening”).**"*\n\nGrade Gain’s printed box supplies the timing the option gives as *4-5 days*: *"Patient with common cold usually has symptoms increasing in **3 or 4 days** to a plateau then recovery occurs … if patient starts to recover and all of a sudden gets severe symptoms **maybe after 2 or 3 or 5 days** → these features point to development of acute bacterial rhinosinusitis"* (`entqb-nose4-156`). Grade Gain also keys double sickening as the **most important** of the four (`entqb-nose4-167`) — it is the most **specific**, because a biphasic course is something a single viral illness does not do.\n\n| Option | Why it fails |\n|---|---|\n| **Never lasts for more than 10 days** | ***Never*** is the word that kills it. Ten days is the **threshold at which persistence starts to suggest bacteria**, not a ceiling on the illness; and *acute* rhinosinusitis is bounded at the other end, at **12 weeks**, which is where `L27.2) Chronic Sinusitis.pdf` starts calling it chronic |\n| **Double sickening within 4-5 days of common cold** | **keyed** |\n| **The majority of cases turn into chronic cases** | flatly contradicted: *"approximately 1 billion cases of viral rhinosinusitis occur each year … of these, only **0.5% to 2.0%** are complicated by bacterial infection"* — and chronicity is rarer still. The deck’s whole argument is that acute rhinosinusitis **resolves** |\n| **All of the above** | cannot stand once two members are false |\n\n**⚠️ A NOTE ON THE STEM’S WORDING, recorded rather than disputed.** The stem asks for a characteristic of *acute rhinosinusitis*, but double sickening is strictly a characteristic of **acute BACTERIAL** rhinosinusitis — the deck prints it under the viral-versus-bacterial heading, not under the definition of acute disease. **The key is right on the option set, since the other three are false outright**, and the distinction is worth carrying: most acute rhinosinusitis is viral and has **no** double sickening.\n\n**⚠️ And what is NOT on the list of four: the colour of the discharge.** Purulent rhinorrhoea is a normal stage of a cold (`L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf`: watery → mucopurulent → *"resolution within 5-10 days"*) and is the commonest reason an antibiotic is prescribed unnecessarily (`entqb-nose4-167`).\n\n**Bottom line: worse after five days · beyond ten days · out of proportion · better then worse.** Applied at 4 days it gives `enthd-nose-331`; at 10 days it gives `enthd-nose-333`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'The four features suggesting bacterial rhinosinusitis are worsening after five days, persistence beyond ten days, symptoms out of proportion, and double sickening — of which double sickening is the most specific.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.95. The four-feature list and the 0.5–2 % conversion rate are transcribed from `L27.1) ACUTE rhinosinusitis.pdf`; the 12-week boundary from `L27.2) Chronic Sinusitis.pdf`; the natural history of the cold from `L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf`. The *3–4 day plateau* timing that the option’s *"4-5 days"* reflects is taken from Grade Gain’s printed box at `entqb-nose4-156`. **⚠️ Recorded, not disputed: double sickening is strictly a feature of acute BACTERIAL rhinosinusitis, while the stem asks about acute rhinosinusitis generally; the key stands because the other three options are false.**' }
,

{ id:'enthd-nose-331', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-sinusitis',
  stem:'An **18-year-old female** patient presents to the rhinology clinic with a complaint of **severe headache together with cough and colored nasal discharge**. She reports that she had a **common cold infection 4 days ago**. What’s your provisional diagnosis?',
  options:['Acute bacterial sinusitis','Acute bacterial rhinosinusitis','Chronic bacterial rhinosinusitis','Acute viral rhinosinusitis'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**Acute viral rhinosinusitis — on day 4 of a cold, with no relapse after an improvement and no disproportion, none of the four bacterial criteria has been met, and coloured discharge is not one of them.**\n\n**⚠️ THIS IS ONE HALF OF A DELIBERATE PAIR AND THE PAIR IS THE TEACHING DEVICE.** The bank prints the **same vignette with the same four options** later, changing only *4 days* to *10 days*, and keys it **acute bacterial rhinosinusitis**. The one variable is the calendar. The rule is set out here in full because the sibling points back at it.\n\n**The rule, in the deck’s own words.** `L27.1) ACUTE rhinosinusitis.pdf`:\n\n> *"Factors suggesting acute bacterial sinusitis are: • **Worsening of the symptoms after five days.** • **Persistence of symptoms for more than 10 days.** • **Presence of symptoms out of proportion** to those typically associated with viral URTI. • **Worsening of symptoms after initial improvement (“double-sickening”).**"*\n\nGrade Gain’s printed box gives the shape of the normal curve those criteria are measured against: *"Patient with common cold usually has symptoms increasing in **3 or 4 days to a plateau** then recovery occurs. **If symptoms persist more than 10 days = acute bacterial rhinosinusitis.** Or if patient starts to recover and **all of a sudden gets severe symptoms** maybe after 2 or 3 or 5 days → these features point to development of acute bacterial rhinosinusitis"* (`entqb-nose4-156`).\n\n**Day 4 is the plateau — the worst day of a normal cold.** `L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf` gives the natural history as *"fever, headache, malaise · dryness of nose · sore throat · sneezing · nasal obstruction · **watery rhinorrhea** · **mucopurulent rhinorrhea** · **resolution within 5-10 days**"*. Every symptom in this stem is on that list.\n\n**⚠️ THE COLOURED DISCHARGE IS THE TRAP, AND IT IS THE MOST EXPENSIVE MISTAKE IN THE WHOLE LECTURE.** Purulence is a **stage of the ordinary cold**, not evidence of bacteria: Grade Gain keys mucopurulent discharge as the *wrong* answer against double sickening (`entqb-nose4-167`). `L27.1` puts numbers on the cost — *"approximately 1 billion cases of viral rhinosinusitis occur each year in the United States; of these, only **0.5% to 2.0%** are complicated by bacterial infection"*, while acute bacterial rhinosinusitis is *"the **fifth most frequent reason for antibiotic prescription** in the United States"*.\n\n| Option | Why not, at day 4 |\n|---|---|\n| **Acute bacterial sinusitis** | wrong on **two** counts — not bacterial yet, **and the wrong name.** `L27.1` opens by insisting the nose is part of it: *"the nasal mucosa is **contiguous** with that of the paranasal sinuses; **any inflammation of the sinuses is almost always accompanied by inflammation of the nasal cavity**"*, so the correct term is always ***rhino*sinusitis**. This distinction decides the sibling question, whose menu prints both spellings |\n| **Acute bacterial rhinosinusitis** | the right name, the wrong day — this is the key at 10 days |\n| **Chronic bacterial rhinosinusitis** | needs **≥12 weeks** (`L27.2) Chronic Sinusitis.pdf`); this is 4 days |\n| **Acute viral rhinosinusitis** | **keyed** |\n\n**Bottom line: the treatment that follows is reassurance, analgesia and time (`enthd-nose-317`), and the safety-net is the four criteria — worse after five days, past ten days, out of proportion, or better-then-worse.** The 10-day version of this same patient is `enthd-nose-333`; the general statement of the rule is `enthd-nose-330`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Four days into a cold, with coloured discharge but no double sickening and no persistence past ten days, the diagnosis is acute VIRAL rhinosinusitis — and the correct term is rhinosinusitis because nasal and sinus mucosa are continuous.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.95. **⚠️ DELIBERATE CONTRAST PAIR with the bank’s Q348 (later batch): the identical vignette at 10 days with the identical menu, keyed acute BACTERIAL rhinosinusitis — recorded in `house-sweep-nose.md`. Do not merge; the 10-day / double-worsening rule is written out in full here and the sibling points back at it.** The four criteria, the contiguity statement, the 0.5–2 % rate and the antibiotic-prescription rank are transcribed from `L27.1) ACUTE rhinosinusitis.pdf`; the cold’s natural history from `L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf`; the 12-week boundary from `L27.2) Chronic Sinusitis.pdf`; the plateau timing from Grade Gain’s box at `entqb-nose4-156`.' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-332', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-sinusitis',
  stem:'A **10-year-old female** patient presented with **pain localized between the eyes**, headache and nasal discharge. What’s your provisional diagnosis?',
  options:['Acute frontal sinusitis','Acute ethmoidal sinusitis','Acute sphenoidal sinusitis','Acute maxillary sinusitis'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**Acute ethmoidal sinusitis — the ethmoid labyrinth lies between the orbits, immediately deep to the bridge of the nose, so its pain is felt exactly where the stem puts it.**\n\nSinus pain is referred to the surface over the sinus, and the corpus prints the whole map in one place (`entep-nose-72`):\n\n| Sinus | Pain referred to |\n|---|---|\n| Maxillary | **cheek**, often felt in the upper teeth |\n| Frontal | **forehead** |\n| **Ethmoid** | **bridge of the nose and between the eyes** |\n| Sphenoid | **occiput / vertex**, retro-orbital |\n\n**The age is the second half of the answer, and it is not decoration.** The **ethmoid air cells are pneumatised from birth**, whereas the **frontal sinus barely exists before adolescence** and the **sphenoid pneumatises late** *(the developmental timetable is not taken from the course material — no cached deck prints it)*. In a 10-year-old, the ethmoid is the sinus that is fully there to be infected. The corpus records the same reasoning from the complication side: **orbital** complications in children come from the ethmoid across the thin lamina papyracea (`entep-nose-34`, `entep-nose-104`, `enthd-nose-325`).\n\n| Option | Why not here |\n|---|---|\n| **Acute frontal sinusitis** | pain over the **forehead**, classically *"vacuum headache … morning pain over the forehead due to obstruction of the frontal recess during sleep"* (`L32) nasal symptoms 2.pdf`), and an unlikely sinus in a 10-year-old |\n| **Acute ethmoidal sinusitis** | **keyed** |\n| **Acute sphenoidal sinusitis** | the deepest sinus — **occipital or vertex** pain, often retro-orbital, and characteristically **no facial tenderness at all**, which is why it is the one that gets missed. `L27.1) ACUTE rhinosinusitis.pdf` illustrates acute sphenoiditis only in its *"do we need surgery?"* series |\n| **Acute maxillary sinusitis** | pain and tenderness over the **cheek**, below the inferior orbital margin, often referred to the upper jaw (`entep-nose-85`, `enthd-nose-338`) |\n\n**⚠️ THE QUESTION TO ASK NEXT IN THIS CHILD IS ABOUT THE EYE.** Ethmoid sinusitis in a child is the classic prelude to orbital cellulitis: `L27.1` prints the endoscopic sign to look for — *"pus in the middle meatus"* — and Grade Gain’s box states the rule as *"child with eye swelling during respiratory tract infection = complicated rhinosinusitis"* (`entqb-nose4-160`). **Lid swelling, proptosis, painful eye movement or reduced vision turn this from an outpatient prescription into an urgent CT and admission** (`entep-nose-99`).\n\n**Bottom line: between the eyes = ethmoid, and in a child the ethmoid is both the commonest sinus infected and the most dangerous one.**\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Pain between the eyes localises to the ethmoid sinus, which is also the only sinus fully pneumatised in a child — and the one whose complications cross the lamina papyracea into the orbit.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.95. *"Vacuum headache"* is transcribed from `L32) nasal symptoms 2.pdf`; *"pus in the middle meatus"* and the acute sphenoiditis slide from `L27.1) ACUTE rhinosinusitis.pdf`. **The sinus-to-site pain map is printed by no cached deck; it is taken from the corpus item `entep-nose-72` in preference to outside knowledge, and the sinus development timetable is tagged in the explanation as outside the course material.** Cross-references: `entep-nose-34`, `entep-nose-104`, `entqb-nose4-160`, `entep-nose-99`, `entep-nose-85`.' }
,

{ id:'enthd-nose-333', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-sinusitis',
  stem:'A **22-year-old female** patient had **common cold 10 days ago**. Now she developed **fever, nasal obstruction, mucopurulent rhinorrhea and cheek tenderness**. What’s your provisional diagnosis?',
  options:['Acute viral maxillary sinusitis','Acute bacterial maxillary sinusitis','Acute viral ethmoiditis','Acute bacterial ethmoiditis'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**Acute bacterial maxillary sinusitis — the menu varies two things, the organism class and the sinus, and the stem fixes each of them with one phrase.**\n\n**Which organism class — *10 days ago*.** `L27.1) ACUTE rhinosinusitis.pdf` names the threshold in its list of features suggesting bacterial disease: *"• Worsening of the symptoms after five days. • **Persistence of symptoms for more than 10 days.** • Presence of symptoms out of proportion to those typically associated with viral URTI. • Worsening of symptoms after initial improvement (“double-sickening”)."* A cold that began ten days ago and is now producing **fever** and worsening symptoms has crossed it. Grade Gain states the same rule flatly: *"if symptoms persist more than 10 days = acute bacterial rhinosinusitis"* (`entqb-nose4-156`).\n\n**Which sinus — *cheek tenderness*.** The **anterior wall of the maxillary sinus lies immediately below the inferior orbital margin**, covered only by the soft tissue of the cheek; it is the one sinus that can be pressed on directly, and pressing on it is the bedside test (`entep-nose-85`). Ethmoid pain sits **between the eyes** instead (`enthd-nose-332`).\n\n| Option | Which half is wrong |\n|---|---|\n| **Acute viral maxillary sinusitis** | organism class — at day 4 this would be the answer (`enthd-nose-331`); at day 10 with fever it is not |\n| **Acute bacterial maxillary sinusitis** | **keyed** |\n| **Acute viral ethmoiditis** | both — wrong class and wrong sinus |\n| **Acute bacterial ethmoiditis** | sinus — ethmoiditis gives pain between the eyes and, in a child, an orbital complication |\n\n**⚠️ THE MUCOPURULENT DISCHARGE IS NOT WHAT MAKES THIS BACTERIAL, and the point survives even though the key here is bacterial.** Purulence is a normal stage of a cold (`L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf`: watery → mucopurulent → *"resolution within 5-10 days"*), and Grade Gain keys it as the **wrong** answer when set against double sickening (`entqb-nose4-167`). **It is the calendar and the fever, not the colour, that do the work.**\n\n**What follows.** `L27.1` prescribes an adequate dose rather than a sequence of agents — its worked case of an under-dosed 10-year-old ended in admission and endoscopic drainage with a sterile culture — and Grade Gain keys **amoxicillin or amoxicillin-clavulanate for 10 days** (`entqb-nose4-157`). Intranasal corticosteroids are an adjunct: *"recommended for the treatment of ARS, both in moderate (monotherapy) and severe (with oral antibiotics) disease"*. **No CT is needed** unless a complication is suspected (`enthd-nose-334`).\n\n**Bottom line: past ten days, or better-then-worse, is bacterial; the site of the tenderness names the sinus.**\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Ten days after a cold, with fever and cheek tenderness, the diagnosis is acute bacterial maxillary sinusitis — the calendar decides viral versus bacterial and the site of tenderness decides the sinus.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.95. The four bacterial features, the improper-dosage case and the intranasal-corticosteroid recommendation are transcribed from `L27.1) ACUTE rhinosinusitis.pdf`; the cold’s natural history from `L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf`; the 10-day rule as a sentence and the antibiotic choice from Grade Gain’s boxes at `entqb-nose4-156` and `entqb-nose4-157`. The anterior maxillary wall as the site of tenderness is taken from the corpus item `entep-nose-85`. Cross-references: `enthd-nose-331` (the same rule applied at 4 days), `enthd-nose-330`, `enthd-nose-332`.' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-334', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-sinusitis',
  stem:'**CT nose and paranasal sinuses is NOT carried out** in which of the following situations?',
  options:['Recurrent acute sinusitis despite adequate medical treatment','Chronic rhinosinusitis','Acute rhinosinusitis','Acute rhinosinusitis with complications'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**Plain, uncomplicated acute rhinosinusitis — the one setting in which the deck says the scan is not needed, in exactly those words.**\n\n`L27.1) ACUTE rhinosinusitis.pdf`, on the slide headed *"Do We Need Computed tomography?"*:\n\n> *"It is **not essential to diagnose uncomplicated acute rhinosinusitis**. It is certainly an important diagnostic tool in **assessing for potential complications of acute sinus disease** or **in anticipation of surgery on the sinuses**."*\n\nThat single slide keys the question and disposes of the other three options, because each of them is one of the two indications it names.\n\n| Option | Which indication it satisfies |\n|---|---|\n| **Recurrent acute sinusitis despite adequate medical treatment** | ***in anticipation of surgery*** — repeated attacks that resist proper treatment raise the question of an anatomical block at the ostiomeatal complex, and the CT is the map for the operation that answers it (`enthd-nose-324`). The same clinical setting is one of the four in which `L27.1` also justifies a **culture**: *"recurrent acute rhinosinusitis · acute exacerbation of chronic rhinosinusitis · immunosuppression · failure to respond to appropriate initial antibiotic therapy"* |\n| **Chronic rhinosinusitis** | `L27.2) Chronic Sinusitis.pdf` requires inflammation to be **documented**, and accepts *"radiographic imaging showing inflammation of the paranasal sinuses"* as one of the three ways; Grade Gain goes further — *"investigations is important in chronic rhinosinusitis (**mandatory for diagnosis**)"* (`entqb-nose4-158`). Plain, non-contrast, **coronal** (`enthd-nose-323`) |\n| **Acute rhinosinusitis** | **keyed — the exception.** Diagnosed clinically by the major/minor criteria (`enthd-nose-320`) |\n| **Acute rhinosinusitis with complications** | ***assessing for potential complications*** — the deck’s own surgical series illustrates an *"orbital complication"*, and endpoint keys urgent **CT nose and paranasal sinuses** for a 10-year-old whose lid swelled after a week of sinusitis (`entep-nose-99`) |\n\n**⚠️ READ THE OPTION LIST AS A PAIR OF NEAR-TWINS.** Options c and d differ by two words — *with complications* — and that phrase is the whole difference between an unnecessary scan and an urgent one. **Any orbital sign (lid swelling, proptosis, painful or restricted eye movement, reduced vision) or any neurological sign (severe headache, vomiting, personality change, seizure) converts option c into option d immediately** (`entqb-nose4-160`, `entqb-nose4-161`).\n\n**Bottom line: do not image an ordinary acute sinusitis; image it the moment you suspect it has left the sinus, and image it before you operate.**\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'CT of the sinuses is not needed for uncomplicated acute rhinosinusitis; it is indicated for suspected complications, before surgery, for recurrent disease resisting treatment, and for chronic rhinosinusitis.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.96. The CT slide, the four indications for culture and the orbital-complication surgical case are transcribed from `L27.1) ACUTE rhinosinusitis.pdf`; the documentation requirement from `L27.2) Chronic Sinusitis.pdf`; *"mandatory for diagnosis"* from Grade Gain’s box at `entqb-nose4-158`. The list of orbital and neurological red flags is drawn from the corpus items `entqb-nose4-160` and `entqb-nose4-161` rather than outside knowledge. Cross-references: `entep-nose-99`, `enthd-nose-320`, `enthd-nose-323`, `enthd-nose-324`.' }
,

{ id:'enthd-nose-335', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-sinusitis',
  stem:'Which of the following best describes the **nasal discharge of odontogenic sinusitis** (sinusitis of dental origin)?',
  options:['Bilateral odorless','Bilateral offensive','Unilateral odorless','Unilateral offensive'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**Unilateral and offensive — one tooth, one antrum, and anaerobes.**\n\n**Unilateral, because the source is a single tooth.** `L31) nasal symptoms 1.pdf` teaches the laterality rule directly: unilateral disease *"usually points to structural abnormality or **uni-lateral pathology** (antrochoanal polyp, **odontogenic pathology**)"*, while bilateral disease *"may be mucosal, structural, or mixed"*. The roots of the upper second premolar and first molar lie against the floor of one maxillary sinus (`entep-nose-24`, `enthd-nose-326`), so a periapical abscess, a root canal or an extraction seeds **that** antrum and no other.\n\n**Offensive, because the organisms are oral.** The same deck files *"Odontogenic sinusitis"* second on its list under ***"Nasal Discharge: Foetid"***, after foreign bodies and before sinonasal neoplasms and fungal infections — while ordinary rhinosinusitis appears under *"Mucopurulent / purulent"*, which is a different heading. `L32) nasal symptoms 2.pdf` prints the negative as a True/False item to be answered **false**: *"Odontogenic sinusitis is characterized by **watery** discharge."* The smell comes from **oral anaerobes** rather than the respiratory pathogens of an ordinary sinusitis, and the patient often notices it themselves as **cacosmia** — endpoint defines that as *"bad odour felt by the patient"* (`entep-nose-55`).\n\n| Option | The error |\n|---|---|\n| **Bilateral odorless** | both halves wrong — this is the pattern of an ordinary viral or allergic rhinitis |\n| **Bilateral offensive** | right smell, wrong laterality; a **bilateral** foetid discharge with crusting in a roomy nose is **atrophic rhinitis (ozaena)** instead — and there the smell is *"bad odor from the nose"* noticed by **others** while the patient is anosmic (`L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf`, `entqb-nose3-145`) |\n| **Unilateral odorless** | right laterality, wrong character; **unilateral and *watery*** is the CSF-leak question (`L31)`, `entep-nose-73`) |\n| **Unilateral offensive** | **keyed** |\n\n**⚠️ THE RULE, WITH ITS AGE CLAUSE — endpoint prints it as a box and the second half is the half that is dropped:** *"Unilateral offensive fetid discharge **in adult** = sinusitis of dental origin till proved otherwise ▪ if the same complain **from a child** = **foreign body**"* (`entep-nose-54`, `entep-nose-18`). In an older adult whose one-sided discharge is **blood-stained** and will not settle, add **maxillary malignancy** (`entep-nose-30`).\n\n**Bottom line: unilateral + foetid + adult = look in the mouth.** Treatment covers both compartments — broad-spectrum antibiotics **with a dental opinion**, because antibiotics alone leave the tooth in place (`entqb-nose4-168`).\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Odontogenic sinusitis produces a unilateral, offensive (foetid) nasal discharge — unilateral because one tooth infects one antrum, offensive because the organisms are oral anaerobes.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.96. The laterality rule, the foetid-discharge list and the mucopurulent heading are transcribed from `L31) nasal symptoms 1.pdf`; the *"watery discharge"* True/False item from `L32) nasal symptoms 2.pdf`; ozaena from `L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf`. The unilateral-offensive rule with its age clause is quoted from endpoint’s printed box at `entep-nose-54`. **⚠️ Not a duplicate of `entep-nose-54`** — recorded in `house-sweep-nose.md` under *one replaced distractor is a NEW question*: same key, a different four-option menu. Cross-references: `entep-nose-55`, `entep-nose-18`, `entep-nose-30`, `entqb-nose3-145`, `entqb-nose4-168`.' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-336', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-sinuscomp',
  stem:'**T / F:** Complications occur **more frequently in acute rhinosinusitis than in chronic rhinosinusitis**.',
  options:['True','False'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**True — and it is counter-intuitive, which is exactly why it is examined.**\n\n**⚠️ A TRUE/FALSE MENU CARRIES NO CONTENT, and the cached decks carry none on this topic either** — the ENT slide set runs `L27.2) Chronic Sinusitis` straight to `L29) Rhinitis 1` with no complications file, and *orbital*, *Pott* and *cavernous* all return zero in both rhinosinusitis decks. **The statement is therefore adjudicated from the course’s own second bank rather than from outside knowledge.** Grade Gain prints it in an explanation box, in the course of rejecting the distractor *"only occur in long standing neglected cases"* (`entqb-nose4-162`):\n\n> *"D. is not correct as complications occur in **acute and chronic** rhinosinusitis. But **more on top of acute** rhinosinusitis."*\n\n**Why the intuition is wrong.** The instinct is that damage accumulates, so long-standing disease must be the dangerous one. The opposite holds, and the reason is that **acute** and **chronic** rhinosinusitis are different processes *(the mechanism is not taken from the course material)*:\n\n| | Acute rhinosinusitis | Chronic rhinosinusitis |\n|---|---|---|\n| Process | **virulent organisms under pressure** in a closed, obstructed sinus | low-grade inflammation of the mucosa; `L27.2` calls it *"chronic inflammatory disease of sinus mucosa lasting more than 12 weeks"* and says the aetiology *"remains unclear"* |\n| Pressure | rising — pus in a cavity with a blocked ostium | vented, and often draining |\n| Barriers | intact bone, but crossed by **valveless veins**; in a child the lamina papyracea is thin and incompletely ossified | fibrosis and remodelling, with time for the body to wall the process off |\n| Typical patient | healthy, often a **child**, with **no previous sinus history** | adult with years of symptoms |\n\n**⚠️ THE PRACTICAL CONSEQUENCE IS THE WHOLE POINT: the patient who develops an orbital or intracranial complication is usually the one nobody was worried about.** Grade Gain’s companion box says it as a rule of thumb — *"child with eye swelling during respiratory tract infection = complicated rhinosinusitis"* (`entqb-nose4-160`) — and complications are commoner in children than adults (`enthd-nose-325`, `entep-nose-34`). **Waiting to see whether a swollen eye settles is the error this fact exists to prevent.**\n\n**Bottom line: acute over chronic, children over adults, and a collection is drained rather than treated medically** (`enthd-nose-321`).\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Complications arise on top of ACUTE rhinosinusitis more often than chronic — a closed, pressurised sinus in a previously healthy patient, most often a child.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.96 (printed as a True/False item). **⚠️ NOT COVERED BY ANY CACHED LECTURE** — the ENT set has no complications-of-rhinosinusitis file (no L28), and a True/False menu carries no content of its own. Adjudicated from Grade Gain’s printed box at `entqb-nose4-162`, quoted verbatim, in preference to outside knowledge; the 12-week definition and *"etiology and pathogenesis remain unclear"* are from `L27.2) Chronic Sinusitis.pdf`; the acute-versus-chronic mechanism table is tagged in the explanation as outside the course material. Cross-references: `entqb-nose4-160`, `entep-nose-34`, `enthd-nose-321`, `enthd-nose-325`.' }
,

{ id:'enthd-nose-337', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-facialpain',
  stem:'Which of the following **characterizes frontal sinusitis headache**?',
  options:['It’s maximum in the morning and decreases gradually till it disappears by noon','It’s due to negative pressure (vacuum) in the sinus lumen','It increases by leaning forwards and straining','All of the above','None of the above'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**All of the above — the three options are the three halves of one named entity, and the deck prints the name and the mechanism in a single parenthesis.**\n\n`L32) nasal symptoms 2.pdf`, on its headache slide:\n\n> *"Acute sinusitis causes **facial pain** rather than headache. This includes **“vacuum headache”** (**morning pain over the forehead** due to **obstruction of the frontal recess during sleep**)."*\n\nThat one sentence contains options a and b at once. **The mechanism:** the frontal recess closes overnight while the patient lies flat, the trapped air in the sinus is absorbed by the mucosa, and the resulting **negative pressure** pulls on a mucoperiosteum whose sensory supply is the ophthalmic division of the trigeminal — a deep, boring frontal pain. **The diurnal pattern follows from the mechanism:** once the patient is upright the recess opens, the sinus ventilates and drains, and the pain fades through the morning. Grade Gain keys *"frontal headache"* as *"**vacuum headache**"* outright (`entqb-nose5-172`), and endpoint builds a whole vignette on the pattern — a headache *"worst in the morning and improves by the afternoon"*, worse on bending forward, keyed to sinus disease (`entep-nose-84`).\n\n**Option c is the posture sign, printed by the other bank as a criterion of sinusitis pain.** Grade Gain lists the criteria as **dull aching**, **increase with straining** and **localized over the sinus**, with *throbbing* as the odd one out (`entqb-nose4-164`). Leaning forward or straining raises the pressure of retained secretions against the sinus walls — which is why `enthd-nose-319` describes a patient whose forehead pain is *"exaggerated on bending forwards while doing the prayers"*.\n\n| Option | Status |\n|---|---|\n| **Maximum in the morning, gone by noon** | true — the diurnal half of *"vacuum headache"* |\n| **Due to negative pressure (vacuum) in the sinus lumen** | true — the mechanism the deck names |\n| **Increases by leaning forwards and straining** | true — *"increase with straining"*, `entqb-nose4-164` |\n| **All of the above** | **keyed** |\n| **None of the above** | refuted by any single row |\n\n**⚠️ THE CAUTION THE SAME SLIDE PRESSES HARD, and it is more useful than the question itself:** *"Rhinogenic headache is **controversial**. The most common type of headache in ENT patients is **primary headache** (tension headache, migraine, cluster headache…). These headaches **may be triggered by nasal irritation** (stimulation of the trigeminal nerve)."* So a headache plus a nose does **not** make sinusitis; it is the **posture and the diurnal pattern**, together with obstruction and discharge, that do. The deck is equally sceptical of *"contact headache"* from a septal spur touching the lateral wall, calling that *"controversial"* too.\n\n**Bottom line: morning frontal pain that clears by noon and worsens on stooping is a vacuum headache; anything without a postural or diurnal signature is more likely migraine or tension headache with a nose in the room.**\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Frontal sinusitis gives a "vacuum headache" — maximal on waking, easing by noon, caused by negative pressure behind an obstructed frontal recess, and worsened by leaning forward or straining.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.96. *"Vacuum headache"*, the frontal-recess mechanism, the primary-headache caution and the *"contact headache"* scepticism are all transcribed from `L32) nasal symptoms 2.pdf`. The straining criterion is Grade Gain’s `entqb-nose4-164` and the naming of frontal headache as vacuum headache is `entqb-nose5-172`; the trigeminal supply of the frontal mucoperiosteum is standard anatomy and is not spelled out on the slide. Cross-references: `entep-nose-84`, `entep-nose-72`, `enthd-nose-319`.' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-338', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-sinusitis',
  stem:'A patient presented with **common cold, edema of right cheek, dental pain and right earache**. What’s the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Right ASOM','Right maxillary sinusitis','Right cancer maxilla','Pansinusitis'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**Right maxillary sinusitis — one antrum accounts for all four findings, and no other option accounts for more than two.**\n\n**The cold is the cause.** `L27.1) ACUTE rhinosinusitis.pdf` heads its pathophysiology with *"Upper Respiratory tract infection"* and records that *"up to **80%** of these URTIs may be associated with rhinosinusitis"*, because *"the nasal mucosa is **contiguous** with that of the paranasal sinuses"*.\n\n**The cheek is the sinus.** The **anterior wall of the maxillary sinus lies immediately below the inferior orbital margin**, under the soft tissue of the cheek — it is the only sinus that can be pressed on directly, and oedema and tenderness there localise the disease (`entep-nose-85`).\n\n**The dental pain is the same antrum, felt through the teeth.** The **superior alveolar nerves** run in the walls and floor of the maxillary sinus on their way to the upper teeth, so antral inflammation is referred to the upper jaw. This is the same anatomy that lets a diseased tooth infect the sinus in the other direction (`entep-nose-24`, `enthd-nose-326`) — the traffic runs both ways.\n\n**The earache is referred, not otological**, and the nerve is the subject of the next item (`enthd-nose-339`).\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Right ASOM** | acute suppurative otitis media would give a **red, bulging, immobile drum** and **local** otalgia — and it explains neither the cheek oedema nor the dental pain. ⚠️ **It is the natural first thought and the trap: an ear that hurts with a normal drum is referred pain** (`entep-ear-116`) |\n| **Right maxillary sinusitis** | **keyed** |\n| **Right cancer maxilla** | wrong tempo and wrong company. Malignancy is insidious, in an older patient, with **blood-stained** unilateral discharge, loosening teeth, cheek numbness or proptosis (`entep-nose-30`; `L26) nasal masses.pdf` alarm list) — not something that appears during a cold |\n| **Pansinusitis** | *pan-* is all the sinuses, which cannot produce a strictly **right-sided** picture localised to one cheek |\n\n**⚠️ THE SEQUENCE IS THE EXAMINABLE PART: cold → ostial obstruction → one antrum → cheek + teeth + ear.** Every element radiates from the maxillary sinus, and the ear is the furthest point of that radiation.\n\n**Bottom line: the cheek names the sinus, and the teeth and ear are both referred pain from it.** Management is that of acute bacterial rhinosinusitis if the criteria are met (`enthd-nose-330`, `entqb-nose4-157`); if the discharge is **unilateral and foetid** and there is a bad tooth, it is odontogenic and needs a dental opinion as well (`enthd-nose-335`, `entqb-nose4-168`).\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'A cold followed by cheek oedema, upper dental pain and earache on the same side is acute maxillary sinusitis — the antral walls carry the superior alveolar nerves and the otalgia is referred, not otological.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.96. The URTI pathophysiology, the 80 % figure and the contiguity statement are transcribed from `L27.1) ACUTE rhinosinusitis.pdf`; the unilateral alarm list from `L26) nasal masses.pdf`. The anterior maxillary wall as the site of tenderness and the superior alveolar referral are taken from the corpus item `entep-nose-85`. **⚠️ Chained: `enthd-nose-339` asks the nerve along which this patient’s otalgia is referred and restates the case.**' }
,

{ id:'enthd-nose-339', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-otalgia',
  stem:'*[The bank prints this as “the previous case”; it is restated so the question stands alone:]* A patient with **common cold, edema of the right cheek, dental pain and right earache** has **right maxillary sinusitis**. His **otalgia is referred along which of the following cranial nerves**?',
  options:['Trigeminal','Glossopharyngeal','Vagus','Hypoglossal'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The trigeminal — and this key is correct, not a divergence from the corpus item that keys the glossopharyngeal for a different source of pain.**\n\n**The principle first.** `L17,18) Otorrhea and otalgia.pdf` defines referred otalgia as pain from *"a lesion in a **remote area having the same N.S**"* — the same nerve supply — and records that it accounts for **5 %** of otalgia against **95 %** local. It then prints the four nerves that carry it: ***"Trigeminal Nerve · Glossopharyngeal · Vagus · 2nd & 3rd cervical nerves."*** **The list is in the deck; the map of territory to nerve is on a picture slide (*"NS of the ear"*) and is not printed as text, so it is supplied below from the corpus’s own keyed items in preference to outside knowledge.**\n\n| Nerve | Its ear territory | A lesion here refers to the ear | Corpus item |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Trigeminal (V)** | pinna, anterior external canal and outer drum, via the **auriculotemporal** branch of V3 | **nose and paranasal sinuses**, **upper teeth**, **TMJ**, floor of mouth, nasopharynx | `enthd-ear-3` (nasopharynx), `entqb-ear1-21` (dental caries), `entqb-ear6-158` and `entqb-ear6-178` (TMJ), `entep-mfe5-42` (floor of mouth) |\n| **Glossopharyngeal (IX)** | middle ear mucosa, via the **tympanic branch (Jacobson’s nerve)** | **tonsil and oropharynx** — tonsillitis, quinsy, **post-tonsillectomy** | `entep-throat-109`, `entqb-thr2-46`, `entqb-thr4-201` |\n| **Vagus (X)** | posterior canal wall and concha, via the **auricular branch (Arnold’s nerve)** | **larynx and hypopharynx** — pyriform sinus and supraglottic cancer | `entep-throat-24`, `entep-throat-103` |\n| **C2 and C3** | postauricular skin | **cervical spine** | `entqb-ear1-22` |\n\n**Why V here.** The **maxillary sinus and the upper teeth are both territory of the maxillary division (V2)**, so an inflamed antrum and an aching upper molar refer along the same nerve that supplies the pinna and outer canal through V3. **The dental pain in the stem is the same referral, arriving at a different destination.**\n\n**⚠️ WHY THE ANSWER CHANGES WITH THE SOURCE, AND WHY `entep-throat-109` IS NOT A CONTRADICTION.** That item asks about *"severe ear pain following surgical removal of inflamed tonsils"* and keys the **glossopharyngeal**, because the **tonsillar bed is IX territory** and IX also supplies the middle ear through Jacobson’s nerve. **Referred otalgia has no single nerve — it has four, and the question is always *where is the lesion*, never *where is the pain*.** Sinus and teeth → V; tonsil and oropharynx → IX; larynx and hypopharynx → X; neck → C2/C3. **Both keys are right, and they are right for opposite reasons.**\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **Trigeminal** | **keyed** — the maxillary sinus is V2 |\n| **Glossopharyngeal** | correct for the **tonsil**, not the sinus (`entep-throat-109`) |\n| **Vagus** | correct for the **larynx and hypopharynx** (`entep-throat-24`) |\n| **Hypoglossal** | **purely motor** to the tongue — it carries no general sensation and cannot refer pain anywhere. It appears in three of these menus as the free elimination |\n\n**⚠️ AND THE RULE THAT MATTERS AT THE BEDSIDE:** an ear that hurts with a **normal canal and a normal drum** has referred pain, and the search moves to the nose, sinuses, teeth, TMJ, pharynx, larynx and neck (`entep-ear-116`). In an older smoker that search is for a **tumour** until proved otherwise.\n\n**Bottom line: name the lesion’s territory and the nerve names itself.** Antecedent case: `enthd-nose-338`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Otalgia from maxillary sinusitis refers along the trigeminal nerve, because the sinus and the upper teeth are V2 territory and the pinna and outer canal are V3 — while tonsillar pain refers along IX and laryngeal pain along X.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.97. **⚠️ Back-references “the previous case” = `enthd-nose-338` (p.96); the case is restated in the stem so the entry stands alone.** The definition of referred otalgia, the 95 %/5 % split and the four-nerve list are transcribed from `L17,18) Otorrhea and otalgia.pdf`; **the deck’s territory map is a picture slide and prints no text, so the nerve-to-territory table is built from the corpus’s own keyed items** (`enthd-ear-3`, `entqb-ear1-21`, `entqb-ear6-158`, `entqb-ear6-178`, `entep-mfe5-42`, `entep-throat-109`, `entqb-thr2-46`, `entep-throat-24`, `entep-throat-103`, `entqb-ear1-22`) in preference to outside knowledge; the named branches (auriculotemporal, Jacobson’s, Arnold’s) are standard anatomy not spelled out on any slide. **⚠️ NOT A DIVERGENCE FROM `entep-throat-109`, which keys the glossopharyngeal for post-tonsillectomy otalgia — a different source of pain and therefore a different pathway; recorded in `house-sweep-nose.md`.**' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-340', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-sinusitis',
  stem:'Which of the following is the **most important step in treatment of chronic rhinosinusitis**?',
  options:['Antibiotics according to culture and sensitivity','Corticosteroid therapy','Surgery','Eradication of predisposing factors'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**Eradication of predisposing factors — chronic rhinosinusitis persists because something keeps it going, and treating the mucosa while the cause is still there buys weeks, not cure.**\n\n`L27.2) Chronic Sinusitis.pdf` builds the disease out of its triggers: *"Etiology and pathogenesis **remain unclear** … proposed triggers include **allergens** (e.g. aspirin) · **bacteria** (especially Staph. aureus endotoxins) · **viruses** · **infection of an adjacent organ (e.g. teeth)** · **trauma** · **anatomical variations**"*, and adds that *"**biofilms and smoking** are linked to worse outcomes and resistance to treatment; **smoking also increases CRS risk**"* and that one should *"consider **secondary causes** of CRS in patients unresponsive to treatment"*. **Every one of those is something to remove, and the deck ties non-response back to them explicitly.** `L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf` puts the same principle first in a treatment list — its management of simple chronic rhinitis reads *"**Correcting predisposing factors** · nasal wash · topical +/- systemic steroids · ABCS"* — and `L31) nasal symptoms 1.pdf` states it as a rule: *"Treatment of **The cause (main line)**"*.\n\n**⚠️ A DISCREPANCY WORTH RECORDING, AND THE KEY STANDS EITHER WAY.** `L27.2` uses the word *mainstay* for something else: *"**The mainstay of treatment is: saline nasal irrigation** … **topical intranasal corticosteroids (INCS)**."* On the deck’s own wording, **option b is the mainstay**. The two are not in conflict so much as answering different questions — INCS is the mainstay of **symptom control**, while removing the cause is what makes the control last — but a student who has memorised the `L27.2` treatment slide has a real case for option b, and it should be seen rather than argued away. **The key is recorded as printed and not disputed.**\n\n| Option | Its real place in the plan |\n|---|---|\n| **Antibiotics by culture and sensitivity** | narrow. `L27.2` allows *"chronic antibiotics (especially of the **macrolide** class) … in treatment of CRSsNP"*, and `L27.1) ACUTE rhinosinusitis.pdf` restricts culture itself to four settings — recurrent disease, acute exacerbation of chronic disease, immunosuppression, and failure of appropriate initial therapy. Against a **biofilm**, culture-directed antibiotics under-perform |\n| **Corticosteroid therapy** | the deck’s **mainstay**, with saline — INCS first, a short oral course *"only in CRSwNP"* if they fail, and biologicals in CRSwNP. **The strongest rival option, see the note above** |\n| **Surgery** | explicitly **last**: *"surgery is considered **after failed medical therapy** … requires **at least 12 weeks** of nasal saline, INCS, and often systemic steroids"*, or **>2–3 courses of oral steroids a year** (`entep-nose-17`, `entqb-nose4-159`). And its goals are themselves anti-causal — *"remove obstruction, reduce inflammation, and improve delivery of topical meds"* |\n| **Eradication of predisposing factors** | **keyed** |\n\n**What eradication actually means, item by item:** stop smoking; treat allergy and, in aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease, address the trigger; **treat the tooth** in odontogenic disease (`enthd-nose-327`, `entqb-nose4-168`); correct the anatomical block at the ostiomeatal complex, which is what *functional* endoscopic sinus surgery means (`enthd-nose-322`, `enthd-nose-324`); control reflux; and reconsider the diagnosis in the non-responder — `L27.2` names **vasculitis and immunodeficiency** and sends you to ANCA and ACE, and its CT has the standing duty *"to exclude aggressive infections or neoplastic disease that might mimic CRS"*.\n\n**Bottom line: in chronic rhinosinusitis, ask what is keeping it chronic before deciding what to prescribe.**\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Chronic rhinosinusitis is treated by removing what perpetuates it — smoking, allergy, a diseased tooth, an anatomical block, a missed systemic disease — with saline and intranasal steroids as the mainstay of symptom control and surgery only after twelve weeks of failed medical therapy.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.97. The trigger list, the biofilm and smoking sentence, the secondary-causes advice, the mainstay of medical treatment, the macrolide indication, the surgical threshold and the goals of surgery are all transcribed from `L27.2) Chronic Sinusitis.pdf`; *"correcting predisposing factors"* from `L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf`; *"treatment of the cause (main line)"* from `L31) nasal symptoms 1.pdf`; the four culture indications from `L27.1) ACUTE rhinosinusitis.pdf`. **⚠️ DISCREPANCY RECORDED, NOT DISPUTED: `L27.2` calls saline irrigation plus intranasal corticosteroids "the mainstay of treatment", which is option b; the printed key is option d and is kept as printed, with the tension explained in the entry.** Cross-references: `entep-nose-17`, `entqb-nose4-159`, `entqb-nose4-168`.' }
